=== layout: one-panel
# Bateson and Punnett
@body

- In 1909, Bateson and Punnett decided two cross two varieties of pea, each of which was pure-breeding for [white flower]{color=gray}
- This cross yielded an F1 generation in which all progeny had [purple flowers]{color=purple}.
  - Next, two F1 plants were crossed to create the F2 generation.
- In this generation, Bateson and Punnett counted a total of 382 [purple-flowered]{color=purple} plants and 269 [white-flowered]{color=gray} plants.
  - The ratio of [purple flowers]{color=purple} to [white flowers]{color=gray} was thus 9.4\:6.6, or approximately 9\:7.

=== layout: two-panels
@left

![Slide image 6](assets/lect05h-epistasis/image_006.png)
@right

- [Experiment:]{color=orange} [Bateson and Punnett, 1909]{color=blue}
- P: true-breeding white x white
- F1: all purple flowers
- Self- or cross-pollination of F1
- F2: 382 purple; 269 white
- Approximate 9:7 ratio (9.2:6.5)

=== layout: one-panel
# Bateson and Punnett
@body

- In 1909, Bateson and Punnett decided two cross two varieties of pea, each of which was pure-breeding for [white flower]{color=gray}
  - For the F1 generation, all progeny had [purple flowers]{color=purple}.
  - For the F2 generation, they found a ratio of [purple flowers]{color=purple} to [white flowers]{color=gray} of approximately 9\:7.
- Bateson proposed an ability of an "allelomorphic pair" (pair of gene alleles) to mask the affects of the alleles for another gene.
  - It seemed that two recessive alleles at one flower locus could mask the effects of the alleles at the other flower locus.

=== layout: one-panel
# Bateson and Punnett
@body

- Let's designate the first locus as the C locus, and the second as the P locus.
  - If Bateson's theory held true, it meant that any flower with the cc genotype would be white, no matter what alleles were present at its P locus.
  - Similarly, any flower with the pp genotype would also be white, no matter what alleles were present at its C locus.
- Bateson later used the word "epistasis," which translates as "standing upon," to define the masking action of one gene by another.

=== layout: one-panel

# Bateson and Punnett: biochemical explanation

@body

- Genes identified much later: Dooner et al., 1991
- Two-step anthocyanin synthesis produces purple pigment
- Gene C: first reaction; gene P: second reaction
- Dominant C and P: functional steps
- Recessive c and p: nonfunctional steps
- cc or pp blocks pigment synthesis: white flowers

=== layout: one-panel
# What are the Phenotypes?
@body

- The dominant C and P alleles code for functional steps in anthocyanin production, whereas the recessive c and p alleles code for nonfunctional steps.
  - Thus, if two recessive alleles occur for either gene, white flowers will result.

=== layout: one-panel
# What are the Phenotypes?
@body

- The dominant C and P alleles code for functional steps in anthocyanin production, whereas the recessive c and p alleles code for nonfunctional steps.
  - Thus, if two recessive alleles occur for either gene, white flowers will result.

=== layout: one-panel
# What are the Phenotypes?
@body

- The dominant C and P alleles code for functional steps in anthocyanin production, whereas the recessive c and p alleles code for nonfunctional steps.
  - Thus, if two recessive alleles occur for either gene, white flowers will result.
- 9\:7 ratio

